      "problem": "Consider a function $h(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{h(t)\\}=H(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{5t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-5)t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $H(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-5)t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t=H(s-5),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-5>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+5.\n$$",
